{:edb/db-before-t 2143 :edb/db-after-t 2144 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535456 50 #inst "2026-10-14T01:33:40.100Z" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186052094 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052095 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186053001 1078 #edb/ref 17592186053002 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186054693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506561 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055121 1071 "A solution to the linear equation $y = 3x - 2$ is the ordered pair $(4, t)$. What is the value of $t$?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186055121 1071 "A solution to the linear equation $y=3x-2$ is the ordered pair $(4,t).$ What is the value of $t?$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055121 1074 "Since $(4,t)$ is a solution to the linear equation $y=3x-2,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $4$ and $t$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $4$ and $y$ with $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-2 \\\\ (t) & =3(4)-2 \\\\ t & =12-2 \\\\ t & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055294 1071 "Which of the following ordered pairs is **NOT** a solution to $y = \\frac{x}{4}$?\n\n1. $(-8,-2)$\n2. $(4, 1)$\n3. $(-4, 1)$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186055294 1071 "Which of the following ordered pairs is **NOT** a solution to $y=\\frac{x}{4}?$\n\nI. $(-8,-2)$\n\nII. $(4,1)$\n\nIII. $(-4,1)$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186055294 1074 "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-8,-2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ (-2) & =\\frac{-8}{4} \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(-8,-2)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(4,1)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ (1) & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a true statement, so $(4,1)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(-4,1)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ (1) & =\\frac{-4}{4} \\\\ 1 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(-4,1)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is *not* a solution to the equation is III $(-4,1).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186057607 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186057609 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186057610 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186057611 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505938 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506312 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506329 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186057606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506331 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186057606 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506329 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058273 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058274 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058275 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058276 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058277 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506016 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506159 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506235 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506323 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506355 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186058272 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506355 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059458 1071 "For the function $g(x)$ defined on the set of integers, find $g(-12)$ and $g(3)$, where $g(x)$ is the following piecewise function:\n${2x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 3x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059458 1071 "For the function $g(x)$ defined on the set of integers, find $g(-12)$ and $g(3),$ where $g(x)$ is the following piecewise function:\n![](images/43/43ec07dcf63fdaf3f85bd576c64da4f4afccee23143cfe51c337dd3e3e855cce.svg)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059458 1074 "- First, we compute $g(-12)$. Since $-12 < 1$, we use the first branch:\n$g(x)|= 2x; g(-12)|= 2(-12); =-24$\n- Next, we compute $g(3)$. Since $3 \\geq 1$, we use the second branch:\n$g(x)|= 3x; g(3)|= 3(3); = 9$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059458 1074 "- First, we compute $g(-12).$ Since $-12 < 1,$ we use the first branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =2x \\\\ g(-12) & =2(-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute $g(3).$ Since $3 \\geq 1,$ we use the second branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x \\\\ g(3) & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059459 1071 "Given that\n![](images/cf/cfe7164dbaeba6215a7fcb84fd9bf192a19c6165562ef124344258df3fcafde6.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059459 1071 "Given that\n${3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3$\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)$?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059459 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, -3)$, we have $f(x) = 3$, so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(-3,3)$, excluding this point.\n- On the interval $[-3, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) = 1$, so the graph is a horizontal line that begins at $(-3,1)$, including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0cdcbeb59ce6f011504ead0de090fa79c925fb933b1a99ce2f885e61eb33107.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059459 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, -3),$ we have $f(x) = 3,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(-3,3),$ excluding this point.\n\n- On the interval $[-3, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) = 1,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that begins at $(-3,1),$ including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0cdcbeb59ce6f011504ead0de090fa79c925fb933b1a99ce2f885e61eb33107.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059608 1071 "If $f$ is the piecewise function\n![](images/e6/e6a8c403594625a24b7f5af7eb5909d626e0ffbba5fc9a4947b7d856013c2bfe.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059608 1071 "If $f$ is the piecewise function\n${-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)$?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059608 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, 0]$, we have $f(x) = -1$, so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(0,-1)$, including this point.\n- On the interval $(0, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) =-x+2$, so the graph is a straight line of slope $-1$ that starts from $(0,2)$, not including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47a6d2d5b84424d0347efc7d9dc64ee90323855b233fde68827bff2a820121af.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059608 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, 0],$ we have $f(x) = -1$, so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(0,-1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(0, \\infty)$, we have $f(x) = - x+2$, so the graph is a straight line of slope $-1$ that starts from $(0,2),$ not including this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47a6d2d5b84424d0347efc7d9dc64ee90323855b233fde68827bff2a820121af.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059623 1074 "The function is defined for values between $-4$ and $1$.\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=-4$, this point is not included in the domain.\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-1$, this point is included in the domain.\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=1$, this point is not included in the domain.\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-4$ and $x=1$, not including $x=-4$ and $x=1$ but including $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n$x\\in (-4,1)$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=-1$, even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=-1$ is $2$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059623 1074 "The function is defined for values between $-4$ and $1.$\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=-4,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ not including $x=-4$ and $x=1$ but including $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-4,1)\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=-1,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=-1$ is $2.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506523 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506601 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506574 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506586 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506531 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506576 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059893 1050 540.0 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060191 1071 "![](images/1a/1a7b132b3efcd909ec61421c956c6808cdbfd813123e2e9341fd0116d0d3deda.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y = f(x)$ shown above?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186060191 1071 "![](images/1a/1a7b132b3efcd909ec61421c956c6808cdbfd813123e2e9341fd0116d0d3deda.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060191 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have a line with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $1.$ So the graph is the line $y=2x+1$ that ends at $(1,3),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have a line with a slope of $-2$ that passes through the point $(1,0).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-0=-2(x-1)\\,⟹\\,y=2-2x\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=2-2x$ that starts at $(1,0),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060275 1074 "The function is defined  for values less than $-1,$ and between $-1$ and $4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=4,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there are two open circles for $x=-1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,4].\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060870 1064 "A **piecewise function** is a function that is defined differently in different parts of the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the piecewise function ![](images/a8/a8e8a9c3f9a7bef1c9c40cad27eeb93267b8b30eb74ce36acd9bf90b461c21ae.svg) defined as follows:\n![](images/78/78daf51ed18581f874f275e211d9f6b651af383503fdbc6e04f7d06b083eaffd.svg)\nA plot of the function is given below:\n\n![](images/2a/2ad96db45424ed30eb3219a4ae36e13d5498a3719695ccb73a9313058f2fa15c.png)\n\nThe function has two distinct branches, one for  ![](images/a3/a3d6121723fd4e9feea862013f28e4326de8afabb08b46040ca1c80788500f84.svg) and one for  ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg). The function is a piecewise function because it has different definitions depending on where we are in the domain. \n\nSuppose that we want to calculate ![](images/0c/0c0dc4f7a3b289c14dd126e050458eab6b82ccccb923d9c6993ac5042303568e.svg) For this, we'd use the  left branch ![](images/a3/a3d6121723fd4e9feea862013f28e4326de8afabb08b46040ca1c80788500f84.svg) because ![](images/85/8521c0711d7afa3552209b26e08d80787124feea347c2cdfa81a8de5470ab58d.svg) On this branch, the function definition is ![](images/5d/5d344346082f57f1cd7456779e9a30d079068f78a466fe899ac376cfbd14dbaf.svg) so we have\n\n![](images/0e/0e990ed7688ceb39db8d63fd54c0f22f52f5260efb6636419319b92b43171610.svg)\n\nSimilarly, if we want to calculate ![](images/71/713ad3a0e19cfab825d702bd00e14c956db1844974c9da945d697930a02e7178.svg), we'd use the  right branch ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg) because ![](images/95/95a3686bf6cd3fddc29b98a39ab23027d1cc794ae2517aefde675a62f0a4d023.svg) On this branch, the function definition is ![](images/85/85f568b3e2d870a755a97fb3457f3c62e814f559b8b017ac05c291fb5d8aa79e.svg) so we have\n\n![](images/16/166c6a001bb66b83e3d369c2211f11baf60650e54c93da7bee47539fa717d44a.svg)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060870 1064 "A **piecewise function** is a function that is defined differently in different parts of the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the piecewise function $f(x)$, defined as follows:\n\n$$\n{-2x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0\n$$\n\nA plot of the function is given below:\n\n![](images/11/111157ad82daacd5cbca8095c9fa1645ea9920e9bb9b26e8588e23dc492bca0d.png)\n\nThe function has two distinct branches, one for\n\n$$\n\\color{red}{x\\leq 0}\n$$\n\nand one for $\\color{blue}{x>0}$. The function is a piecewise function because it has different definitions depending on where we are in the domain.\n\nSuppose that we want to calculate $f(-1)$. For this, we'd use the left branch\n\n$$\nx \\leq 0\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nOn this branch, the function definition is\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\nf(-1) = -2(-1) = 2\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we want to calculate $f(1)$, we'd use the right branch $x > 0$ because $1 > 0$. On this branch, the function definition is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\nf(1) = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186062617 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062622 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064146 1071 "Consider the function $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x$. Its derivative is given by\n\n$y'(x) = x^4 (\\cos x)^x \\left(5 + g(x) \\right)$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064146 1071 "Consider the function $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\ny'(x) = x^4 (\\cos x)^x \\left( 5 + g(x) \\right).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064146 1074 "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{5}) + \\ln ((\\cos x)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 5\\ln x + x\\ln (\\cos x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(5\\ln x + x\\ln (\\cos x)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= \\frac{5}{x} + (x)^{′} \\cdot \\ln (\\cos x) + x \\cdot (\\ln (\\cos x))^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) + x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x {:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x} + \\ln (\\cos x) - x\\tan x) \\\\\n&= x^{5}(\\cos x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x} + (x\\ln (\\cos x))/(x) - \\frac{x^{2}\\tan x}{x}) \\\\\n&= x^{4}(\\cos x)^{x}(5 + x ln ⁡ (cos ⁡ x) - x 2 tan ⁡ x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = x \\ln (\\cos x) - x^2 \\tan x\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064146 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{x}+(x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x {:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = x \\ln ( \\cos  x ) - x^2 \\tan x.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064147 1071 "Consider the function $y = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}$. The derivative of this function is given by\n$y' = -\\dfrac{g(x)(x+3)^2}{(x+5)^7}$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064147 1071 "Consider the function $y = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{g(x)(x+3)^2}{(x+5)^7}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064147 1074 "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (((x + 3)^{3})/((x + 5)^{6})) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((x + 3)^{3}) - \\ln ((x + 5)^{6}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 3\\ln (x + 3) - 6\\ln (x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(3\\ln (x + 3) - 6\\ln (x + 5)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x + 3} - 6 \