7592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186083676 1074 "Remember that the graph of $y=|x-a|$ is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $a$ units right.\n\nHere, the graph is moved *right* by $2$ units. This corresponds to $a=2.$ So, the equation of the graph is $y=|x-2|.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c305a89fe9fd400cd9c58cfb12522b143b2654a49fa58aee582ba1100f94daa.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186083931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186083931 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $((y - 1)^{2})/(16) - ((x - 3)^{2})/(4) = 1$?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186083931 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186083931 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=3$ and $k=1.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(3,1).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186084526 1064 "Let's plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\n. According to the algebraic definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n{x\\begin{vmatrix}forx \\ge 0 \\\\ -x\\end{vmatrix}forx < 0\n$$\n\nSo, the left-hand branch of the graph is the function $y=-x$, and the right-hand branch is $y=x$:\n\n![](images/60/60ad4dfdb6086f20f5137c5b4ac4905e55d5697ec4cf31575bf79be5fd408e0c.png)\n\nNotice that the graph has a sharp corner at $(0,0)$. We call this point the **vertex** or the **minimum point.**\n\nWe can apply **horizontal** and **vertical translations** to the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\n.\n\n- The graph of $y= \\mid x \\mid +a$ is a **vertical translation** of the absolute value graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$. It moves the graph $a$ units up.![](images/bc/bc56f0d75b8359f71a104580902a18a1d8009091b94694beb7f8ac71c9bfc9bc.png)\n- The graph of $y= \\mid x-a \\mid$ is a **horizontal translation** of the absolute value graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$. It moves the graph $a$ units to the *right*.![](images/1f/1f79063f7350e734811366545507fc44c321882d2d9b228b85475a27d46c1759.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When drawing horizontal and vertical translations, it is easiest to transform the graph's vertex first. Then, the two branches can be drawn coming out of the vertex.\n- When we translate the graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$, the resulting curve is called the **image** of $y = \\mid x \\mid$ under the translation." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084526 1064 "Let's plot the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg). According to the algebraic definition of absolute value, we have\n![](images/eb/ebc6cc8f3964043ffa2ff15f7e3501222794753f30577e4b71037efb2e4ffa30.svg)\nSo, the left-hand branch of the graph is the function ![](images/8f/8f96aa73ce843c7815a1fed6bf3f3774a173cfa4d56f13e1f3a8c48d53cd7097.svg), and the right-hand branch is ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg):\n\n![](images/60/60ad4dfdb6086f20f5137c5b4ac4905e55d5697ec4cf31575bf79be5fd408e0c.png)\n\nNotice that the graph has a sharp corner at ![](images/c1/c1e8c20213b45135f50bdc8d4544220f7b0f943ff3ca49439660c86d017e789f.svg) We call this point the **vertex** or the **minimum point.**\n\nWe can apply **horizontal** and **vertical translations** to the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg).\n\n- The graph of ![](images/91/91c9b5c48d46f81336e889febab90a9027a96accef8626bd0743ca81042f25b8.svg) is a **vertical translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units up.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8b826034f7c3668cacb490e0fab60daaf7c5cae63975bfa5cae9d468c7dec6.png)\n\n- The graph of ![](images/30/3016548be8be38ace3450d51a10371543820a3357b1adbf809d69c23c70f14bb.svg) is a **horizontal translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units to the *right*.\n\n![](images/65/65912d2831dd525964b78a20f2a707d7d828daa2a5e2ebd0793c6b337a42e360.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When drawing horizontal and vertical translations, it is easiest to transform the graph's vertex first. Then, the two branches can be drawn coming out of the vertex.\n\n- When we translate the graph ![](images/87/87f4b5227cb69bd468d75d826fe448c91ae677181f9142fcbe9bc757d0acea77.svg) the resulting curve is called the **image** of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg) under the translation." 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186084528 1064 "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x-h \\mid +k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nby\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084528 1064 "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=|x-h|+k$ is a translation of the graph $y=|x|$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up." 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084597 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084598 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084599 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084600 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084601 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186084596 1075 #edb/ref 1759