$\\angle B$.\n\n![](images/d9/d91fc772042490e0785a4a617e4c3785c8616b7ed0ff05509ca8bc902eccc2a8.png)\n\nAnother way to construct an exterior angle of $\\angle B$ is to extend $\\overline{CB}$ past $B$. The angle between $\\overline{AB}$ and the extension of $\\overline{CB}$ is also an exterior angle of $\\angle B$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d031a6a6cc1513fc0a8031755b537d144279b88c964fc75728d3588ae1e4af9.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105471 1074 "To construct an exterior angle of $\\angle B,$ we extend $\\overline{AB}$ past $B.$ The angle between $\\overline{BC}$ and the extension of $\\overline{AB}$ is an exterior angle of $\\angle B.$\n\n![](images/d9/d91fc772042490e0785a4a617e4c3785c8616b7ed0ff05509ca8bc902eccc2a8.png)\n\nAnother way to construct an exterior angle of $\\angle B$ is to extend $\\overline{CB}$ past $B.$ The angle between $\\overline{AB}$ and the extension of $\\overline{CB}$ is also an exterior angle of $\\angle B.$\n\n![](images/8d/8d031a6a6cc1513fc0a8031755b537d144279b88c964fc75728d3588ae1e4af9.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105472 1071 "![](images/02/02ac83b8318d6067ba7959c390ccb8b011001ebcb289e4f31c27ba8e1ceb2913.png)\n\nIn the figure above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle{F}$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105472 1071 "![](images/02/02ac83b8318d6067ba7959c390ccb8b011001ebcb289e4f31c27ba8e1ceb2913.png)\n\nIn the figure above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle{F}.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105472 1074 "Let us call $x$ the exterior angle of $\\angle F$. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100^{∘} + 60^{∘} + 78^{∘} + 62^{∘} + x &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n300^{∘} + x &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 360^{∘} - 300^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105472 1074 "Let us call $x$ the exterior angle of $\\angle F.$ Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{100}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{78}^{∘}+{62}^{∘}+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {300}^{∘}+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={360}^{∘}-{300}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105473 1071 "![](images/0f/0f2d7f92335d965840f8714dc4ceeb43201ecee2ada9b7aeb7eb54437a0f7d0f.png)\n\nFind the value of $x$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105473 1071 "![](images/0f/0f2d7f92335d965840f8714dc4ceeb43201ecee2ada9b7aeb7eb54437a0f7d0f.png)\n\nFind the value of $x.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105473 1074 "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $A$ is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ - 90^\\circ=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand the measure of the exterior angle at $D$ is $180^\\circ - 2x$.\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90^{∘} + (x + 10^{∘}) + (180^{∘} - 2x) + x + (x + 10^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nx + 290^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105473 1074 "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $A$ is $180^\\circ - 90^\\circ=90^\\circ$ and the measure of the exterior angle at $D$ is $180^\\circ - 2x.$ \n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ},$  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{90}^{∘}+(x+{10}^{∘})+({180}^{∘}-2x)+x+(x+{10}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{290}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105474 1071 "![](images/22/22130cbd3bb1f36d9ef8ce6290eead15354bbced7810e5fad5ac62972cd1d968.png)\n\nFor the quadrilateral above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle A$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105474 1071 "![](images/22/22130cbd3bb1f36d9ef8ce6290eead15354bbced7810e5fad5ac62972cd1d968.png)\n\nFor the quadrilateral above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle A.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105474 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12x - 5^{∘}) + (6x + 5^{∘}) + (10x) + (9x - 10^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n37x - 10^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n37x &= 360^{∘} + 10^{∘} \\\\\n37x &= 370^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - 5^{∘} &= 12(10^{∘}) - 5^{∘} \\\\\n&= 120^{∘} - 5^{∘} \\\\\n&= 115^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186105474 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12x-{5}^{∘})+(6x+{5}^{∘})+(10x)+(9x-{10}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 37x-{10}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 37x & ={360}^{∘}+{10}^{∘} \\\\ 37x & ={370}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of the exterior angle of $\\angle A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-{5}^{∘} & =12({10}^{∘})-{5}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}-{5}^{∘} \\\\  & ={115}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506537 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506538 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506512 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506556 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506557 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506562 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105975 1050 900.0 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106033 1071 "![](images/62/6252354d464db3c645186b9a8ef6001ee76408b04f7abf6a9046656270607085.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠B$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186106033 1071 "![](images/62/6252354d464db3c645186b9a8ef6001ee76408b04f7abf6a9046656270607085.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠B.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106033 1074 "Let us call $z$ the exterior angle of $∠B.$ Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{50}^{∘}+z+{80}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{100}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {280}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106488 1071 "![](images/ac/ac5d02edc0945574b5bf8f9a1112e9d7e9ca33ebc7714bfadcd14d02e46c43c7.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186106488 1071 "![](images/ac/ac5d02edc0945574b5bf8f9a1112e9d7e9ca33ebc7714bfadcd14d02e46c43c7.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106488 1074 "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ is ${180}^{∘}-(4x-{1}^{∘})={181}^{∘}-4x,$ and the measure of the exterior angle at $E$ is ${180}^{∘}-{86}^{∘}={94}^{∘}.$\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘},$  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{1}^{∘})+(2x+{14}^{∘})+({181}^{∘}-4x)+(2x+{16}^{∘})+{94}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (2x+2x-4x+2x)+({1}^{∘}+{14}^{∘}+{181}^{∘}+{16}^{∘}+{94}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{306}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{1}^{∘} & =2({27}^{∘})+{1}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186107162 1064 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B$.\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\n> The measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ$.\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ$.\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186107162 1064 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186107163 1064 "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ$?\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ)$.\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$.\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$.\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSum of Exterior Angles &= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - (n - 2) \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - n \\cdot 180^{∘} + 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - n \\cdot 180^{∘} + 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186107163 1064 "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ?$\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ).$\n\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}.$\n\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}.$\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Sum of Exterior Angles} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-(n-2)\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}-\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186110777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506540 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186110778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506515 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186110778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506571 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186114915 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that:\n\n- For $x\\in(-2,2)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in(2,5)$ the graph falls\n- For $x\\in(5,7)$ the graph is flat\n- For $x\\in(7,9)$ the graph rises\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n- The function is *strictly increasing* on the intervals where it always rises: $(-2,2)$ and $(7,9)$.\n- The function is *increasing* on the intervals where it never falls: $(-2,2)$ and $(5,9)$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186114915 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in(-2,2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in(2,5)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in(5,7)$ the graph is flat\n\n- For $x\\in(7,9)$ the graph rises\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n- The function is *strictly increasing* on the intervals where it always rises: $(-2,2)$ and $(7,9).$\n\n- The function is *increasing* on the intervals where it never falls: $(-2,2)$ and $(5,9).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186114916 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that:\n\n- For $x\\in (-4,-3)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in (-3,0)$ the graph falls\n- For $x\\in (0,2)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in (2,4)$ the graph is flat\n- For $x\\in (4,5)$ the graph falls\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that:\n\n- The graph is *strictly decreasing* on the intervals where it always falls: $(-3,0)$ and $(4,5)$.\n- The graph is *decreasing* on the intervals where it never rises: $(-3,0)$ and $(2,5)$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186114916 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-4,-3)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (-3,0)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (0,2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (2,4)$ the graph is flat\n\n- For $x\\in (4,5)$ the graph falls\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that:\n\n- The graph is *strictly decreasing* on the intervals where it always falls: $(-3,0)$ and $(4,5).$\n\n- The graph is *decreasing* on the intervals where it never rises: $(-3,0)$ and $(2,5).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506560 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506565 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506510 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506533 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506555 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506569 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115437 1050 420.0 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186116429 1064 "A function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is **strictly increasing** if its ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value always rises as we increase the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/f6/f6136545f441473a1c873f7d3296e8d96c23357825ac442ce83ce313f2d74199.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is **increasing** if its ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value *never falls* as we increase the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. \n\n**Watch Out!** Increasing functions, like the one below, are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/b9/b979741f27906322c7ff290ae37bb5214c0c88d30e504fe18569c1f957e3d72e.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186116429 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **strictly increasing** if its $y$-value always rises as we increase the $x$-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd868f19387999e329288fd1d492c071d3f0e12c4e9b474e092bc913791d013.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function $f(x)$ is **increasing** if its $y$-value *never falls* as we increase the $x$-value.\n\n**Watch Out!** Increasing functions, like the one below, are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/39/39cf4a0a64391114e7b79eed6f039ea4fd0f0772ba2cfb8d4b36c4ab0a5d53b2.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186132475 1078 #edb/ref 17592186132476 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506593 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506514 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506511 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186134406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186134406 1071 "$(-15)^{2}=$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186134406 1071 "What is $(-15)^{2}$?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186134406 1074 "Since the product of two negative numbers gives a positive number, we have\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}=(-15)\\cdot (-15)=15\\cdot 15\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $15\\cdot 15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}={15}^{2}=225.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186147398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506554 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186147398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506580 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186147399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506541 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186157273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506579 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186157273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506584 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186157278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506572 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186157278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506587 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186157279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506592 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186164154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506026 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186164154 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506026 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186166935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506573 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186166935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506578 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167188 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3-2s}{s(s-1)},\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167188 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) = (3 - 2s)/(s(s - 1))$,\n$X_{2}(s) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186167188 1074 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+3\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-1=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2 & ={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s}-2 \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3-2s}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3-2s}{s(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =1-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =1-(\\frac{3-2s}{s(s-1)}) \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{s(s-1)-3+2s}{s(s-1)} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-s-3+2s}{s(s-1)} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+s-3}{s(s-1)} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+s-3}{s(s-1)(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system  in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3-2s}{s(s-1)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+s-3}{s(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167189 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1/2}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3/2}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167189 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=\\frac{1/2}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3/2}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186167189 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s)