on $5{x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186184813 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}x) & =3 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 5[{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $q=\\frac{16}{5}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{15}{5}=3.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186184814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186184814 1071 "The equation $-5x^{2} + 5x - 1 = 0$ can be written in the form $5(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$." 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186184814 1071 "The equation $-5{x}^{2}+5x-1=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186184814 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-5{x}^{2}+5x-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x & =-1 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-x) & =-1 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 5[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186184815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183499 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186190630 1078 #edb/ref 17592186190635 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186191913 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191917 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186191919 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191923 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186192464 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & <5x-6 \\\\ 14+6 & <5x-6+6 \\\\ 20 & <5x+0 \\\\ 20 & <5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & <5x \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & <\\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & <\\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ 4 & <x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember that we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx \\gt 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x  \\gt 4.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b6c834b9f69e2610a3c61bb765ec0cba5ad03cfcd22a3ddb0290ff0fa3882f0.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186192464 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}14 & < 5x - 6 \\\\ 14 + 6 & < 5x - 6 + 6 \\\\ 20 & < 5x + 0 \\\\ 20 & < 5x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20 & < 5x \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & < \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & < \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ 4 & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember that we also need to flip the inequality.\n$x \\gt 4$\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\gt 4$. The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b6c834b9f69e2610a3c61bb765ec0cba5ad03cfcd22a3ddb0290ff0fa3882f0.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186192467 1074 "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-6$. To isolate $a$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-6$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-6$.\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6a & >- 12 \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & <\\frac{-12}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & <2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-6a}{-6} & < 2 \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & < 2 \\\\ a & < 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a< 2$. The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5947166eb517c08355c09c265f6a2c1f87bdafd776b2e03f5503585568b90b.png)" 13194139535456 17592186045417 false] [17592186192467 1074 "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-6.$ To isolate $a,$ we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-6.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-6.$\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-12 \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-12}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6}\\, & <\\,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6a}{-6} & <2 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-6}a}{\\cancel{-6}} & <2 \\\\ a & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<   2.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5947166eb517c08355c09c265f6a2c1f87bdafd776b2e03f5503585568b90b.png)" 1319413