e-55af-af71-359acfb6c6dc" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506043 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506508 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506508 1077 "field-2" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506508 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506043 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506508 1079 #uuid "fdfbe9d8-ba4d-594f-9b79-9657cca29157" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506473 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1069 "q-138850" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1071 "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1072 #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506509 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506459 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1069 "q-27757" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1071 "![](images/7e/7e96cb4b3fe002216cb63ac5394cb31daa62675ae2fe36bf883a8448dea3a150.png)\n\nOn which interval is the function $y=f(x)$, shown above, decreasing?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1072 #uuid "0877512d-738e-5955-a71c-cb46e2b35ffc" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506510 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ the graph rises\n\nThe function is decreasing on the intervals where it never rises: $(-\\infty ,0).$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506401 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1069 "q-89409" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1071 "A fair spinner has three regions, red, blue, and green. Suppose the spinner is spun $21$ times. What is the variance of the number of times the spinner lands on blue?" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1072 #uuid "088aa711-4888-53e6-84c3-09dd07764006" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506511 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=21$ trials\n\n- the probability of landing on blue is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(21,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of times the spinner lands on blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506496 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1069 "q-52907" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1071 "![](images/88/88c545b9f94d8ada244caf648dd4a820ef717258bcd59745a3e5bcf3c71d2167.png)\n\nFor the quadrilateral above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠D.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1072 #uuid "09e9dcd8-fbe7-5f9f-9e34-7eb900193f19" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506512 1074 "Let us call $z$ the exterior angle of $∠D.$ Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{41}^{∘}+{106}^{∘}+{127}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {274}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={86}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506474 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1069 "q-138831" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1072 #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506513 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506478 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1069 "q-40857" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1071 "$(-5.5)^{2}=$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1072 #uuid "14d93e8f-2ff1-562e-a35c-f8de5c3818d2" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506514 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $-5.5$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-5.5)^{2}=(-5.5)\\cdot (-5.5)=(5.5)\\cdot (5.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55\\times 55=3\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $3\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.5}^{2}=30.25.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506464 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1069 "q-53062" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is sometimes not a rectangle.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is never a rhombus." 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1072 #uuid "16005fe9-c0e8-5807-8dbf-172a83d89558" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506515 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In a rectangle, its four angles are right angles. The same is true for a square. A square is a specific type of rectangle, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. A square is a specific type of rhombus, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506494 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1069 "q-141806" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1071 "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1072 #uuid "1724c4ea-89ed-51e2-a2bf-8d02f0f074fa" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506516 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506488 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1069 "q-138800" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1071 "Consider the function $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={3}^{x+4}\\cdot (a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1072 #uuid "174736f7-0588-55bb-bfa7-155dc6d18a73" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506517 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)({x}^{4}+1)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=4+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\n$$" 13194139535456 17592186045417 true] [17592186506518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506418 13194139535456 17592